        "description": "<p>On Thursday, three of the <a href=\"https://investors.raspberrypi.com/leadership\">lead Raspberry Pi engineers</a> hosted an <a href=\"https://www.reddit.com/r/engineering/comments/1tcyfvk/hello_rengineering_were_eben_upton_ceo_james/\">AMA on the r/engineering subreddit</a>.</p>\n<figure class=\"insert-image\"><img src=\"https://www.jeffgeerling.com/blog/2026/news-about-raspberry-pi-6-and-microcontroller-development/pi-reddit-ama.jpg\"\n alt=\"Raspberry Pi Reddit AMA with Eben Upton, Gordon Hollingworth, and James Adams\" width=\"500\" height=\"auto\">\n</figure>\n\n<h2 id=\"raspberry-pi-6\">Raspberry Pi 6</h2>\n<p>One of the most interesting tidbits was on the Pi 6.</p>\n<p>Looking back at previous launches:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>2012: Raspberry Pi</li>\n<li>2015: Raspberry Pi 2 (+3 years)</li>\n<li>2016: Raspberry Pi 3 (+1 year)</li>\n<li>2019: Raspberry Pi 4 (+3 years)</li>\n<li>2023: Raspberry Pi 5 (+4 years)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Following that cycle, one would expect a Pi 6 3-4 years after the Pi 5, which would put it",
